# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for `audio-no-device-degrades`, and ONLY that test.
#
# ⚠ THIS FILE MUST NEVER SUPPRESS ANYTHING GLOAM WROTE. It is wired into exactly
# one ctest case via that case's ENVIRONMENT property in test/CMakeLists.txt —
# never into TSAN_OPTIONS globally, never into a toolchain file. Every other
# suite on the thread leg, including test/15voicering/ and test/28voicemix/ whose
# whole purpose is to be audited by TSan, runs with no suppressions at all.
#
# ── What is suppressed, and why it is not ours ──────────────────────────────
#
# `gloam --audio` calls `DeviceSink::open()`, which constructs an `RtAudio`.
# RtAudio's constructor enumerates devices, and on this platform that reaches
# alsa-lib — whose `default` PCM is routed through a PulseAudio plugin. libpulse
# then spawns its own `threaded-ml` thread, and TSan reports a race between that
# thread and the main one:
#
#   WARNING: ThreadSanitizer: data race
#     Atomic read of size 1 by thread T1:
#       #0 pthread_mutex_lock
#       #1 pa_mutex_lock (libpulsecommon-16.1.so)
#     Previous write of size 8 by main thread:
#       #0 malloc
#       #1 pa_xmalloc (libpulsecommon-16.1.so)
#       #2 RtApi::getDeviceNames()
#   SUMMARY: ThreadSanitizer: data race in pa_mutex_lock
#
# Every frame is inside libpulsecommon or RtAudio's call into it. No GLOAM
# object, no GLOAM thread and no GLOAM memory appears on either side. The
# libraries are uninstrumented, which is why TSan cannot see the synchronisation
# they actually use — this is the classic uninstrumented-third-party report
# rather than evidence of a bug in anything.
#
# It is also intermittent: measured 5 of 6 runs on this dev box, and the Clang
# thread leg passed the whole suite once by luck. An intermittent red on one leg
# is worse than a documented suppression, and worse than either is turning the
# device probe off to please a tool.
#
# ── What this does NOT excuse ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# GLOAM's own audio threading is audited elsewhere and must stay that way:
# test/15voicering/ runs two real threads over the SPSC ring, and
# test/28voicemix/ runs a producer against `Mixer::render`. Both are pure
# gloam::lib / src/bin code with no RtAudio linked, both run on the thread leg,
# and neither loads this file. If a race is ever introduced in the ring or the
# mixer, those are what catch it.
#
# ── One trap, if you edit the patterns below ───────────────────────────────
#
# A `called_from_lib` pattern that matches MORE THAN ONE loaded library is
# FATAL, not a warning. This file first read `called_from_lib:libpulse`, which
# is a prefix of both libraries here, and TSan refused to start:
#
#   ThreadSanitizer: called_from_lib suppression 'libpulse' is matched against
#   2 libraries: '/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libpulse.so.0' and
#   '/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/pulseaudio/libpulsecommon-16.1.so'
#
# The process then exits 66 with no race report at all — which looks exactly
# like the race it was meant to suppress, and cost a second round of diagnosis.
# Patterns must be specific enough to name one library.
#
# A pattern matching ZERO libraries is fine and silent, which is what makes this
# safe on a CI runner that may have no PulseAudio installed: no pulse, no pulse
# thread, no race, and an inert line here. Verified both ways.
#
# Remove this file the day RtAudio's enumeration stops reaching libpulse, or the
# day the distro ships an instrumented libpulsecommon. Do not extend it.

called_from_lib:libpulsecommon
race:pa_mutex_lock
race:pa_xmalloc
